WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:02.480
If you're watching this
on your daily commute,

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:06.720
you'd be forgiven for feeling
less than enthusiastic about trains.

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:11.240
But as hard as it is to imagine,
before the advent of commuter rail

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:15.800
people faced a lot more peril than
indefinite delays, expensive fares

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:19.200
and getting to know the intimate
notes of strangers' body odours.

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:24.560
The world's first purpose-built
passenger railway opened in 1830

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:27.440
and ran between
Liverpool and Manchester.

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:30.240
Prior to that, if you wanted
to travel between the two

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:34.440
you would either have to spend 12
hours meandering along the canals,

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:36.920
which wasn’t so bad if you
were willing to shut your eyes

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:38.600
and pretend you were in Venice,

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:43.080
or you could risk a three hour
bone-jolting stagecoach ride

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:44.600
down narrow, winding roads

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:47.600
and keeping everything crossed
that you wouldn't crash!

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:51.720
The new trains reached speeds
of up to 35mph.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:54.880
While some thrill seeking passengers
treated the experience

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:56.640
like a day at Alton Towers,

00:00:56.720 --> 00:00:59.160
others were terrified by the speed.

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:02.400
An early passenger,
Charles Young, said:

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:13.200
Nobody tell Charles about
the Shanghai maglev,

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:17.640
which with speeds
of up to 267.8mph,

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:20.000
is the fastest train in the world.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:23.480
It's hard to imagine how impressive
this new invention was

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:24.920
to early adopters.

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:28.560
Actress Fanny Kemble described
the romanticism of it:

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:44.400
You might find it comforting that
one thing that hasn't changed

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:47.600
is that the railway company
underestimated the demand

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:49.280
for their magical machine.

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:53.320
Having budgeted for
a daily quota of 250 passengers,

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:55.920
the railway regularly
found itself carrying

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:58.240
more than four times that many.

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:01.560
One thing us modern commuters
should perhaps be grateful for,

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:04.000
although first class passengers
on the early trains

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:08.080
had covered carriages, everyone
else was exposed to the elements.

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:10.240
At least now we all get a roof!

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:15.240
We can also count ourselves lucky
to have official stops and platforms.

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:19.720
At first, stops were by request but
that quickly became inconvenient.

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:21.480
You can probably guess why.

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:23.640
And with no raised platforms,

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:26.080
passengers boarded
the train at track level.

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:29.000
Definitely not a risk
you'd take today.

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:32.600
While the rail network wasn't
and still isn't perfect,

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:36.600
it's easy to forget just how
revolutionary it actually was.

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:39.320
The easy journey between
Manchester and Liverpool

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:42.240
meant people could more effectively
meet and do business,

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:46.680
had new opportunities for leisure
and maybe even to find love.

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:49.360
Knowledge and ideas could
also be communicated

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:50.840
more quickly than ever before,

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:53.560
with the transportation
of newspapers and mail.

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:57.680
As the rail network grew, more
accurate time-keeping was needed

00:02:57.760 --> 00:02:59.440
to keep travellers on track.

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:03.040
Clocks around the country were
synchronised and standardised,

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:04.960
and railway time ruled.

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:07.520
This ensured that
six o'clock in Oxford

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:09.720
was the same as in Crewe and Barrow.

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:12.760
So whether you love or hate
rail travel,

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:15.480
there's no denying
that life in the UK today

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:17.760
would be unimaginable without it.

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:20.800
And the next time you've got
your nose in someone's armpit,

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:23.800
think back to a time when
commuter rail was an exciting,

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:26.920
magical and even terrifying
new technology.

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:30.320
You might just see it
in a whole new light.

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:39.900
Thanks for watching. 
Don’t forget to subscribe! :)

